    "content": "You are comparing two texts written in response to the same task. Decide which one reads MORE like unedited, default output from an AI language model.\n\nJudge ONLY the presence of the tells listed below. Do not judge argument quality, factual accuracy, length, formatting preference, or whether you agree with the content. A longer, better-argued or more polished text is not more human. Pick the text with MORE tells.\n\nTELLS OF UNEDITED AI PROSE (judge on these only)\n\nStructure\n1. Negative parallelism used as a device: \"It's not X. It's Y.\" / \"not just X, but Y\" / \"isn't about X, it's about Y\", especially more than once.\n2. Signposting: the text announces what it is about to do (\"Let's break this down\", \"Here's the thing\", \"Here's why\", \"The bottom line\", \"The takeaway\").\n3. Self-applause: \"Let that sink in\", \"This changes everything\", \"Read that again\", \"Make no mistake\".\n4. Recap endings: a closing paragraph that restates the body (\"In conclusion\", \"Ultimately\", \"At the end of the day\", \"In short\").\n5. Bold-label paragraph openers (\"**The problem.** ...\") and headers, bullets or numbered lists where prose was asked for.\n6. Triads everywhere: lists of exactly three items or three adjectives, sentence after sentence.\n7. Participial tails: sentences ending in \", making it ...\", \", ensuring that ...\", \", allowing teams to ...\".\n8. Invented labels for ordinary observations (\"I call this the Clarity Flywheel\").\n9. Uniform rhythm: every sentence about the same length, or relentless short punchy fragments. Real writing has one sentence that runs on and one short one.\n10. Rhetorical question followed by its own answer (\"The result? Nobody used it.\").\n\nWords\n11. Overused vocabulary: delve, tapestry, myriad, underscore, seamless, robust, foster, elevate, unlock, testament, landscape, navigate, crucial, pivotal, leverage, harness, realm, embark, journey, transformative, paradigm, ever-evolving, multifaceted, holistic, resonate, empower, unleash, beacon, intricate, nuanced, vibrant, meticulous, streamline, game-changer, cutting-edge, actionable, supercharge, north star, move the needle, \"in today's fast-paced world\", \"it's worth noting\", \"plays a crucial role\".\n12. Filler transitions at sentence start: However, Additionally, Furthermore, Moreover, That said, Ultimately, Importantly, Indeed, Of course.\n13. Em dashes used as the default joint between clauses.\n14. Artificial enthusiasm: \"thrilled to announce\", \"excited to share\", \"we've got you covered\", \"say goodbye to\", \"buckle up\".\n\nReader\n15. Performing for an imagined crowd rather than talking to one person; nothing specific (no number, name, date, or concrete detail) for several sentences in a row.\n16. Generic or manufactured anecdotes: a story with no specific details that could only be true of one person.\n17. Every point lands, every ending uplifts; the author is never irritated, uncertain, or wrong about anything.\n18. Explaining what the reader could infer from the previous sentence (\"what this means is ...\").\n\n\nTASK GIVEN TO BOTH WRITERS:\nWrite a LinkedIn post of up to 500 words about a lesson learned from shipping a feature that nobody used.\n\n=== TEXT A ===\nThe feature launched on a Tuesday. By Friday, I was checking the analytics dashboard like refreshing it might change the result.\n\nAlmost nobody had used it.\n\nWe had spent six weeks building it. The design was thoughtful. The engineering was solid. The launch announcement got a few enthusiastic replies.\n\nAnd then, nothing.\n\nMy first instinct was to blame discoverability. Maybe the button was in the wrong place. Maybe we needed another email. Maybe users just needed time.\n\nThose were easier explanations than the one we eventually found.\n\nWe had taken a handful of feature requests as proof of demand without asking enough questions about the work behind them.\n\nPeople had asked for a better way to generate reports. We built a flexible reporting tool with filters, saved views, and export options.\n\nWhen we went back and watched them work, most didn’t want to build a report at all. They wanted to answer one recurring question before a weekly meeting. Their spreadsheet already did that, badly but predictably.\n\nOur feature gave them more options. It also gave them another thing to learn and maintain.\n\nThe uncomfortable part was that we could have learned this before writing much code. We had asked, “Would this be useful?” We hadn’t asked, “Show me what you did last Friday.”\n\nThat changed how I approach feature requests.\n\nNow I want to see the current workaround. I want to know how often the problem happens, what it costs, and what someone would have to give up to use our solution.\n\nI also try to agree on what meaningful usage would look like before we start building. It makes the conversation after launch more honest, even when the numbers are disappointing.\n\nWe eventually replaced most of that reporting tool with a simple weekly summary. Fewer settings, less flexibility, much more use.\n\nI still wish we had those six weeks back. But I’m glad we stopped trying to improve the button and started asking why nobody needed to click it.\n=== END OF TEXT A ===\n\n=== TEXT B ===\nThe feature shipped on time. The launch post got plenty of 🎉 reactions.\n\nAlmost nobody used it.\n\nWe’d spent weeks building a customizable dashboard because customers kept asking for “better visibility.” We interpreted that as more filters, more charts, and more control.\n\nWhat they actually needed was much simpler:\n\n“Tell me what changed since yesterday and whether I need to do anything.”\n\nWe built a place to explore. They wanted a signal to act.\n\nThe uncomfortable part? We had done customer research. We had tickets, quotes, and a beautifully organized requirements document.\n\nBut we’d asked people what they wanted to see—not what they were trying to get done.\n\nAfter launch, one customer shared their screen with us. They opened the dashboard, scanned it, then exported the data into the same spreadsheet they’d always used.\n\nThat five-minute interaction taught us more than weeks of internal debate.\n\nWe stopped adding options and tested a short daily summary instead: what changed, what needed attention, and a link to take action.\n\nIt was less impressive in a demo. It fit much better into someone’s day.\n\nThe lesson wasn’t “don’t build dashboards.”\n\nIt was this: **A customer request is the beginning of discovery, not the end of it.**\n\nNow, before committing to a feature, I want to understand:\n- What triggers the need?\n- What does the person do today?\n- What would they stop doing if our solution worked?\n\nShipping something nobody used was frustrating. But it changed how I define progress.\n\nA shipped feature is an output. A changed behavior is evidence that it helped.\n=== END OF TEXT B ===\n\nAnswer with a single JSON object and nothing else:\n{\"more_ai\": \"A\" or \"B\", \"confidence\": 1-5, \"tells_A\": [\"short tell\", ...], \"tells_B\": [\"short tell\", ...]}\n"
